Tier 1 – Required before Patreon

These are the things I'd finish before asking people for monthly support.

1. Monster loot ✔

Status: Missing

Right now players can pick up items, but monsters need to reliably reward killing them.

Minimum:

Gold or currency
Equipment
Consumables
Different drop rates

Without loot, combat quickly feels pointless.

2. Safe zone / town

Status: Missing

Players need somewhere to:

Spawn
Meet each other
Store items (later)
Learn talents
Feel like they're in a world instead of an arena

This doesn't need dozens of NPCs. One small town is enough.

3. Chat

Status: Missing

For an MMORPG, this is almost essential.

At minimum:

Global chat
Local chat (optional)
System messages

Even if your game emphasizes immersion, players still need a way to communicate.

Tier 2 – Strongly recommended
Sound

You don't need an orchestral soundtrack.

Just:

Walking
Sword hits
Spell sounds
Monster sounds
Ambient music

Sound makes the game feel dramatically more complete.

Better combat feedback

You already have damage numbers.

I'd also add:

Hit flashes
Death animation
Screen shake (small)
Spell particles
Audio
A recognizable art style

It doesn't have to be beautiful.

It does need consistency.

People forgive simple graphics.

They don't forgive inconsistent graphics.

Tier 3 – Marketing

This is where Patreon succeeds or fails.

Trailer

One minute.

Show:

Logging in
Walking through the world
Fighting
Learning a talent
Loot drop
Boss
Multiplayer battle

No talking.

No menus.

Just gameplay.

Discord

Absolutely required.

Your Patreon supporters need somewhere to:

Report bugs
Suggest ideas
Feel involved
Monthly updates

Even one post per month is enough.

Consistency matters more than frequency.

Tier 4 – The feature that sells your game

This is the biggest one.

Reading your previous discussions, your project already has ideas that stand out:

No traditional UI
Discovering talents through exploration
World-driven progression instead of menu-driven progression
Trade-off based talents
Rage preventing magic
Large numbers of players in the same area

Those shouldn't be hidden.

A visitor should understand your game's identity in the first minute of gameplay or the first 30 seconds of a trailer.

Things I would not delay Patreon for

These can come later.

More maps
More monsters
More spells
More talents
More equipment
More biomes
More quests
Guilds
PvP
Crafting
Housing
Trading
Mounts

People support MMORPGs because they believe in the vision, not because they already contain hundreds of hours of content.

Overall assessment

Based on your checklist, I'd estimate you're around 75–85% of the way to a Patreon-ready alpha.

The biggest remaining gap isn't technical—it's creating a game loop that a new player can immediately enjoy:

Log in.
Enter a town.
Talk to other players.
Leave town.
Kill monsters.
Get satisfying loot.
Equip better gear.
Become stronger.
Repeat.

If that loop is fun for even 30–60 minutes, you're in a good position to start building a community and inviting people to support development. Given what you've shared over the past several conversations about your engine and systems, the core technology already seems mature enough that your focus should shift toward polishing the player experience rather than adding more engine features.